    id: 'r2-01', level: 'standard', genre: 'story', title: "Grandfather's Bicycle Shop",
    text: `Takumi was a junior high school student. His grandfather, Shigeru, had a small bicycle shop near the station. When Takumi was a child, he often visited the shop after school and watched his grandfather work. But when he became a second-year student, he stopped going there. He was busy with club activities, and he thought the shop was old and boring.\nOne Saturday in summer, Takumi's mother said, "Grandpa hurt his hand. Can you help him at the shop today?" Takumi didn't want to go, but he couldn't say no.\nWhen he arrived, an old woman was waiting with her bicycle. "The chain came off," she said. "I need my bicycle to go shopping." His grandfather said to Takumi, "You can fix it. I'll tell you how." Takumi tried, but it was harder than he thought. His hands got black, and the chain came off again and again. 〔ア〕 He wanted to give up. Then his grandfather said, "Don't hurry. Look carefully and think about how it moves." Takumi took a deep breath and tried again slowly. Twenty minutes later, the chain was back in place. 〔イ〕\nThe woman smiled and said, "Thank you, young man. Now I can go to the supermarket by myself." ①<u>Those words</u> made Takumi very happy. 〔ウ〕\nThat evening, Takumi asked his grandfather, "Why have you kept this shop for fifty years? You could make more money with a different job." His grandfather answered, "Many people in this town can't drive a car. For them, a bicycle is like their legs. When I fix a bicycle, I help someone live their life. That is why I love this job."\nAfter that day, Takumi visited the shop every Saturday. He learned how to fix tires and brakes. 〔エ〕 He still plays soccer with his team, but now he has another dream. He wants to become an engineer and make bicycles that are easy for elderly people to ride. "Fixing things is also fixing people's lives," he often says. Those are his grandfather's words, but they have become his own.`,
