    id: 'r2-02', level: 'standard', genre: 'explanation', title: 'Small Workers, Big Help',
    text: `Do you like honey? Many people enjoy it on bread or in tea. But honeybees do much more than make honey. They also help us grow a lot of the food we eat every day.\nWhen a honeybee visits a flower, it collects nectar to make honey. At the same time, small pieces of pollen stick to its body. When the bee flies to the next flower, some of the pollen moves to that flower. This helps the plant make fruit and seeds. Apples, strawberries, melons, and many other fruits and vegetables need ①<u>this help</u>. Some scientists say that about one third of the food we eat depends on bees and other insects.\nHowever, the number of honeybees is getting smaller in many parts of the world. There are several reasons. Some farmers use chemicals to kill insects that damage their crops, and these chemicals can hurt bees, too. Also, as towns get bigger, there are fewer fields of flowers. Changes in the weather are another problem. 〔ア〕\nWhat can we do to protect bees? Some people are trying an interesting idea. In cities such as Tokyo and Paris, people keep honeybees on the roofs of tall buildings. 〔イ〕 You may think that cities are bad places for bees, but that is not true. There are many flowers in parks, along streets, and in people's gardens. The bees fly around the city and collect nectar from them. 〔ウ〕 The honey made on these roofs is sold at local shops, and it has become popular.\nThese projects also teach people about nature. In one city, junior high school students visit a roof and learn how bees live. A student said, "I was afraid of bees before. But now I know they are hard workers, and they don't attack us if we stay calm." 〔エ〕\nWe don't need to keep bees to help them. ( A ) Even a few pots of flowers on a balcony can give food to bees. If many people do small things like this, our towns will become better places for bees, and for us, too.`,
