    id: 'r2-05', level: 'standard', genre: 'speech', title: 'The Power of Listening',
    text: `Hello, everyone. Today I'd like to talk about something I learned at a nursing home last summer.\nOur school has a volunteer program, and I chose to help at a nursing home near my house. On the first day, I was very nervous. I didn't know how to talk to elderly people. I thought, "They may not be interested in a junior high school student like me."\nA staff member asked me to talk with a woman named Mrs. Sato. She was ninety years old and sat by the window every day. I said, "Hello. Nice to meet you." But she just looked at me and said nothing. I didn't know what to do, so I talked about the weather and my school. She still didn't say anything. ①<u>I felt sad when I went home that day.</u>\nThe next week, I visited her again. This time, I brought a photo of my town's old station. My grandfather had told me that it was built about eighty years ago. When I showed the photo to Mrs. Sato, her eyes became bright. "I took a train from this station when I was a girl," she said. Then she began to tell me many stories about her life. She talked about her family, her school days, and the songs she sang when she was young. I just listened and asked some questions. We talked for an hour.\nWhen I was leaving, she held my hand and said, "Thank you for listening to me. I haven't talked this much for a long time."\nI realized something important. At first, I tried to talk a lot because I wanted to make her happy. But what she needed was not my talking. She needed someone who would listen to her.\nNow there are many elderly people in Japan, and some of them feel lonely. We young people can't solve every problem. But we can listen. Listening is a small thing, but it can make someone's day brighter. Why don't you talk with an elderly person around you this week? Ask them about their memories. You may learn something wonderful, just like I did.\nThank you.`,
